---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Card from '../components/ui/Card.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="How It Works - Recipe Nutrition Calculator"
	description="See how this recipe nutrition calculator parses ingredients, converts units to grams, matches a USDA-derived database, and calculates calories and macros per serving."
>
	<section class="max-w-[760px] mx-auto px-md sm:px-lg py-3xl space-y-lg">
		<div>
			<h1 class="text-display-lg text-ink mb-sm">How it works</h1>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				We'd rather be transparent about how the numbers are calculated than pretend they're more precise
				than they are.
			</p>
		</div>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">Where the nutrition data comes from</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				Nutrition values come from a curated database of common ingredients, built from public-domain USDA
				reference data. It's not a live lookup against the full USDA database - it's a focused set covering
				the ingredients that show up in the vast majority of home recipes. If an ingredient isn't in our
				database, we fall back to a generic estimate and flag it clearly as{' '}
				<strong>approximate</strong> rather than guessing silently.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">How we parse your recipe</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				Pasted text (and photo transcriptions) are split into ingredient lines and parsed for quantity, unit,
				and ingredient name - handling fractions, ranges, and common unit abbreviations. Each ingredient name
				is then matched against our database using word-overlap scoring. A low-confidence match is shown as{' '}
				<strong>low-confidence</strong> on the ingredient line so you know to double-check it. If an ingredient
				isn't in our database at all, we ask an AI model for a best-guess estimate rather than skipping it -
				those lines are clearly marked <strong>AI-estimated</strong>, since that number isn't sourced from
				verified nutrition data.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">How units convert to grams</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				Weight units (grams, ounces, pounds) convert directly. Volume units (cups, tablespoons) use an
				ingredient-specific density where we have one - flour and water don't weigh the same per cup - and
				count units ("2 eggs", "1 onion") use an average weight for that ingredient where known. When we
				don't have ingredient-specific data, we fall back to water's density for volume or a generic
				per-item weight for counts, and mark that line <strong>approximate</strong>.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 id="raw-vs-cooked-ingredients" class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs scroll-mt-24">Raw vs. cooked ingredients</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				Cooking changes a food's nutrient content - heat degrades some vitamins, water-soluble nutrients
				leach into cooking liquid, and moisture loss concentrates or dilutes others. When your ingredient
				line says something like <span class="font-mono">2 cups cooked rice</span> or{' '}
				<span class="font-mono">1 lb boiled potatoes</span>, we try to match a USDA entry for that cooked
				state rather than a raw one. If a line doesn't specify raw or cooked, we match whichever entry
				scores best, which is often the raw version - so the result may not fully reflect what cooking
				does to that specific ingredient. Stating the prep in your ingredient list (raw, cooked, boiled,
				roasted, etc.) gets you a closer match. Either way, treat the numbers as a solid estimate, not a
				lab measurement.
			</p>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 id="tips-for-each-way-to-add-a-recipe" class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs scroll-mt-24">Tips for each way to add a recipe</h2>
			<div class="space-y-md">
				<div>
					<h3 class="text-body-md-strong text-ink mb-xxs">Paste text</h3>
					<p class="text-body-md text-body">
						One ingredient per line, written as <strong>quantity, unit, name</strong> - e.g.{' '}
						<span class="font-mono">2 cups flour</span> or <span class="font-mono">1 lb chicken breast</span>.
						Combining two ingredients on one line is the most common cause of a wrong result - calculation
						happens as soon as you submit, with no review step in between, so it's worth getting the
						format right first.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div>
					<h3 class="text-body-md-strong text-ink mb-xxs">Manual entry</h3>
					<p class="text-body-md text-body">
						Fill in quantity, unit, and name as separate fields, one ingredient per row. Leave the unit
						blank for counted items (e.g. <span class="font-mono">2</span> eggs), and leave quantity blank
						for to-taste seasonings.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div>
					<h3 class="text-body-md-strong text-ink mb-xxs">URL import</h3>
					<p class="text-body-md text-body">
						We look for the structured "Recipe" data (schema.org JSON-LD) that most recipe sites embed for
						Google's recipe search results - so the URL needs to point straight at one recipe's page, not a
						homepage, category page, or roundup of several recipes. If a site doesn't publish that data,
						URL import won't work for it. Some sites also block automated requests outright (you may see a
						"couldn't fetch that page" error) even when they do publish the data - if that happens, open
						the page yourself and paste the ingredients directly instead.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div>
					<h3 class="text-body-md-strong text-ink mb-xxs">Photo upload</h3>
					<p class="text-body-md text-body">
						Photograph the ingredient list itself - a recipe card, cookbook page, or printout - not the
						finished dish. Printed text transcribes most reliably; keep the shot in focus, well-lit, and
						free of glare. You'll review the transcribed text and can fix any misread lines before
						calculating.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</Card>

		<Card variant="marketing">
			<h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-xs">The health score</h2>
			<p class="text-body-md text-body">
				A simple, transparent 1–10 score starting at 5: it goes down for high sodium, saturated fat, or sugar
				per serving (over 20% of the daily value), and up for a good source of fiber or protein (over 15–20%
				of the daily value). It's a rough signal, not a clinical assessment.
			</p>
		</Card>
	</section>
</Layout>
